-- The join line is column-checked, every word of it (L4.Parser.joinLine).
--
-- Here the author writes `UPON EACH` at the OUTER rule's clause column,
-- meaning it for the outer EVERY -- but a nested EVERY is still open inside the
-- outer's HENCE, and the nested one's clauses live further right. Before the
-- guard (measured 2026-09-07) this parsed clean, attached the fork to the INNER
-- rule, and exactprinted byte-for-byte identically, so nothing showed the author
-- that barrier-versus-fork had been decided by invisible layout -- the one
-- distinction `ContinuationWithoutJoin` refuses to guess at.
--
-- The message you get depends on WHICH word is misplaced, which is worth
-- knowing before you debug one. A misplaced HEAD keyword (ONCE or UPON), as
-- here, falls out of the join slot without consuming anything, so the leftover
-- keyword is reported by the expression follow-set: a long "expecting %, &&, *,
-- ..." list that never says "indentation". Only a misplaced SECOND word (ALL,
-- HAVE, EACH) reports "incorrect indentation (got N, should be greater than M)",
-- because by then the join is committed. Measured 2026-09-07.

DECLARE Actor IS ONE OF
    Tenant HAS name IS A STRING

DECLARE Action IS ONE OF
    Sign HAS signer IS AN Actor

GIVETH A DEONTIC Actor Action
`bad` MEANS
    EVERY Tenant x
        MUST   Sign (EXACTLY x)
        WITHIN 1
        ONCE   ALL HAVE
        HENCE  EVERY Tenant y
                   MUST   Sign (EXACTLY y)
                   WITHIN 2
        UPON   EACH
